Both traces recorded. Summary:

**Subject:** `lmp` (LAMMPS single executable, built from `src/` with the REAXFF package enabled)

**Trace 1: `reaxff-cpu-mpi`** (default REAXFF package build)
- Pure MPI, spatial-decomposition domain code with ghost-atom halo exchange (`ghostneigh=1`, `comm->forward_comm`)
- Charge-equilibration (QEq/ACKS2/QTPIE) solved via CG each timestep: every iteration does forward_comm + reverse_comm (halo) *and* a scalar `MPI_Allreduce` (dot product) — a tight {neighbor, reduction} loop, highly latency-sensitive but not bandwidth-heavy (small per-atom messages)
- No GPU, no shared-memory windows, no parallel I/O library
- Compute-heavy many-body bond-order/valence/torsion kernels dominate FLOPs; the QEq sparse matvec is a classic low-arithmetic-intensity sparse kernel
- Can run single-node or multi-node; wants neighbor-preferring/co-located placement since CG iterations couple ranks tightly every step

**Trace 2: `reaxff-kokkos-cuda`** (Kokkos-accelerated `reaxff/kk` style)
- MPI + GPU (Kokkos CUDA/HIP/SYCL backend), typically one rank per GPU chosen via local-rank env vars (SLURM_LOCALID, OMPI_COMM_WORLD_LOCAL_RANK, etc.)
- Device-resident communication buffers; `gpu_aware_flag=1` by default → direct GPU-to-GPU MPI path when the MPI library supports it (mpi-ext.h CUDA-aware probe included)
- Forces full neighbor lists on GPU (avoids newton's-third-law reverse force communication)
- Same {neighbor, reduction} QEq/CG communication pattern as CPU variant, but kept on-device

Both traces were recorded with evidence citations; the CPU-MPI trace is high confidence (direct code reads of comm/MPI calls), the Kokkos-CUDA trace is medium confidence (strong structural evidence but some behavior — e.g. exact intensity — inferred from flags/comments rather than runtime profiling).
